The environment menu, server side: Stop sandbox, Cancel start, the Pod's
status and Keep running.

Stop sandbox. Stop used to end only the runner: the Pod kept running until its
shutdownTime passed, 30 minutes later in production, and the next message
relaunched the runner on it. stop_session with data.suspend_sandbox now also
suspends the session's managed sandbox the way an idle stop does:
AgentSandboxLauncher.suspend moves spec.shutdownTime to now, the controller
deletes the Pod and keeps the Sandbox and its volume, the host tunnel drops so
the session reads as host_asleep, and the next message wakes it through
resume_managed_host. managed_host_keepalive skips the stopped sandbox for two
minutes and runs the suspend on its own worker, behind any refresh already
queued, so a runner tunnel that hasn't dropped yet can't push the deadline
back. Without the flag stop_session is unchanged; the composer's Restart agent
uses that. A rejected suspend is a 502 whose detail the web UI shows. A
claimed warm-pool Sandbox isn't named by its handle, so its suspend() raises a
capability error rather than patching nothing and reporting success.

Cancel start. Stop sandbox while a stopped sandbox wakes cancels the wake. Its
provider calls run on worker threads and can't be interrupted, so the route
marks the start cancelled (managed_hosts.cancel_managed_start) and publishes
"ready", so the session reads as stopped straight away. Every stage of a start
goes through _publish_sandbox_status, which raises StartCancelledError for a
cancelled start, so the wake fails at its next stage and the message waiting
on it gets "you cancelled the start" instead of being sent, even when the wake
had already passed its last stage. Once the wake has stopped, the cleanup
stops the sandbox again, in case the wake got as far as starting the Pod. A
new session's first launch isn't cancelled, since losing its Pod would delete
the new Sandbox: Stop sandbox answers 409 ("still being created") until it has
started. A new start clears an old cancel that never settled.

Status. GET /v1/sessions/{id}/environment returns when the Pod started, the
Sandbox's spec.shutdownTime (when it stops, or stopped), the idle-stop window,
the VM disk from os.statvfs("/") with the free fraction the kubelet evicts on
(f_bavail / f_blocks), and the runner's latest report: CPU and memory in use
and the agent's idle deadline. The runner posts it to
POST /v1/sessions/{id}/environment/report every 5 seconds; only the session's
bound runner may, by its tunnel token. Reports live in memory and one older
than 30 seconds isn't shown. AgentSandboxLauncher.environment reads the
Sandbox and its Pod, both of which the server's role already allows.

Keep running. PUT /v1/sessions/{id}/environment/keep-running takes
{"hours": n} (up to a week), {"forever": true}, or {} for the usual idle
timeout, owner-only like Stop sandbox, and stores it in the
omnigent.keep_running_until label as "<until>/<hours>" or "forever", so the
menu can tick the choice. The answer to each runner report carries it, and
GET .../environment shows it. A time in the past no longer holds. Stop sandbox
clears it.
Pairs with the runner patch 0011-environment-menu.patch and the web patch
0020-environment-menu.patch.
Applies after 0014-session-subscription.patch. Base: omnigent v0.15.0 (c8b9b85f822f).

